Chapter: MicListen
  cites Kernel chapter MicInput
  cites Kernel chapter HdaAudio

 Ties the machine's ears to its voice: it BEEPS to ask for a sound, then
 listens for several seconds and reports whether it heard anything. The
 beep is the cue -- you do not have to be watching the console. Skipped in
 the battery (needs a real microphone and speaker, and a person to answer).
 Run it by hand:

   build/compile.ps1 -Src codex/test/apps/mic-listen.codex -Out m.cdx -Log m.log
   tools/codex-vm.exe -kernel m.cdx -mem 3072

 The beep plays once and the output stream is stopped so it does not loop
 under the listen window. is-quiet takes ~200 ms per reading, so the
 monitor spans several seconds -- time to hear the beep and answer.

 The row says [Microphone, Audio] because this program does both: it
 speaks and it listens. It used to say [Device.Mmio] for the beep, which
 granted the whole Device family -- the bus and the block device -- to a
 program whose entire ambition was one note. Two narrow authorities that
 name what it actually does is the point.

Section: Body

  monitor : Integer, Boolean -> [Microphone] Boolean
  monitor (rounds) (heard) =
    if rounds <= 0 then heard
    else if is-quiet then monitor (rounds - 1) heard
    else monitor (rounds - 1) True

  opening : [Console, Microphone, Audio] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni "MIC: listen for the beep, then make a sound..."
    b <- hda-play-tone 880 20
    s <- hda-stop
    heard <- monitor 25 False
    print-line-uni (if heard then "MIC: I heard you!" else "MIC: stayed quiet")
  end
